---
import ExpertisePage from '../../../components/ExpertisePage.astro';

const data = {
  eyebrow: 'From Source to Delivery',
  title: 'Connect Responsible Production to Credible Products and Claims',
  lead: 'Terra Nexus helps commodity traders, processors, food companies, retailers, and their partners design sustainable supply chains that align sourcing, supplier engagement, traceability, operations, commercial value, environmental accounting, and customer expectations.',
  heroImage: 'https://images.pexels.com/photos/4487383/pexels-photo-4487383.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1600',
  primaryCta: { label: 'Discuss Your Supply Chain', href: '/contact/' },
  secondaryCta: { label: 'Explore Our Capabilities', href: '/capabilities/' },
  metaTitle: 'Sustainable Supply Chains | Terra Nexus',
  metaDescription: 'Terra Nexus helps commodity traders, processors, food companies, retailers, and their partners design sustainable supply chains that align sourcing, supplier engagement, traceability, operations, commercial value, environmental accounting, and customer expectations.',
  orientation: [
    'A sustainable supply chain is not a supplier questionnaire, a software platform, a certification logo, or a reporting dashboard. It is the system through which products, data, money, responsibilities, environmental attributes, and claims move among producers, originators, processors, manufacturers, brands, retailers, technology providers, assurance organizations, and customers.',
    'A program can have strong upstream outcomes and still fail commercially if the commodity is not sourced reliably, the evidence does not follow the product, the contract does not allocate the attribute, the processing system cannot preserve the claim, or the buyer cannot use the result. Terra Nexus helps clients connect responsible production with the commercial and operating infrastructure required to deliver, substantiate, and capture value.',
    'This Area of Expertise focuses on how food and agricultural products, evidence, attributes, incentives, responsibilities, and value move across participants. It may include responsible sourcing and procurement, traceability and chain of custody, commodity origination and aggregation, processing and allocation, logistics and delivery, supplier and value-chain collaboration, environmental accounting and claims, and resilience and risk management. This topic connects every other Area of Expertise. It explains how upstream change becomes a product, customer outcome, claim, or other form of value.',
    'Traceability is not one thing. A company may need to know the country of origin, the supplying facility, the farm, the field, the production practice, the product batch, the environmental attribute, or only the aggregate performance of a defined supply shed. The right approach depends on the product and supply-chain structure, the business decision or claim, the point at which materials are mixed or transformed, the ability to segregate or reconcile volume, the cost and reliability of data collection, applicable certification or regulatory requirements, the risk of fraud, and whether the claim must remain physically linked to the product. Terra Nexus helps clients select and govern the level of traceability required for the intended use rather than defaulting to the most complex or least costly option.',
    'Commodity traders and originators may connect producers, storage, aggregation, logistics, processors, buyers, environmental attributes, and program data. They can source and aggregate eligible commodities, design or administer producer programs, maintain delivery and transaction records, manage physical and attribute inventories, translate downstream requirements into upstream workflows, structure customer offerings and value sharing, coordinate technology, agronomy, assurance, and program partners, and manage reconciliation and commercial risk. Terra Nexus helps traders determine which role to play, where value is created, what controls are needed, and how to build an offering that can scale.',
  ],
  performanceCategories: [
    { title: 'More Reliable and Resilient Supply', items: ['Greater visibility into sourcing regions, suppliers, and critical dependencies', 'Reduced exposure to concentrated, fragile, or high-risk supply', 'Stronger supplier relationships and contingency planning', 'Improved ability to respond to climate, market, logistics, or policy change', 'More stable quality, availability, and service'] },
    { title: 'Better Supplier and Producer Economics', items: ['Clearer participation requirements and incentives', 'Improved alignment between buyer value and supplier cost', 'More durable contracts and commercial relationships', 'Reduced administrative burden and duplicated data requests', 'Better access to technical support, finance, programs, or markets'] },
    { title: 'Improved Operational Performance', items: ['Reduced waste, inventory, delay, error, or rework', 'Improved planning, logistics, processing, and utilization', 'Better integration of procurement, sustainability, quality, and operations', 'Stronger data quality and exception management', 'More efficient program delivery at scale'] },
    { title: 'Credible Evidence and Claims', items: ['Traceability proportionate to the intended decision', 'Clear product and attribute ownership', 'Consistent calculation and allocation boundaries', 'Documentation that can withstand customer, auditor, certification, or regulatory scrutiny', 'Reduced risk of double counting, unsupported claims, fraud, or reconciliation gaps'] },
    { title: 'Stronger Commercial Value', items: ['Differentiated products, ingredients, feedstocks, or customer programs', 'Access to preferred-supplier, certification, regulatory, or procurement pathways', 'Improved margin, customer retention, market access, or strategic positioning', 'Monetization or use of environmental attributes', 'Improved resilience, supply security, and enterprise risk management'] },
  ],
  systemSteps: [
    { title: 'Define the Business Objective and Claim Boundary', body: 'The starting objective may be responsible sourcing, supplier risk, Scope 3, certification, a low-carbon product, regulatory eligibility, resilience, customer reporting, product differentiation, or cost and service improvement.', detail: 'Terra Nexus helps define the product, geography, supplier population, value-chain boundary, intended outcome, user of the evidence, and level of assurance.' },
    { title: 'Map the Physical, Commercial, and Data Flows', body: 'A useful supply-chain map shows more than where the product moves. It also shows who owns the material at each stage; where it is stored, mixed, transformed, or allocated; who controls producer and supplier relationships; which contracts govern product and attributes; where data are created, changed, or lost; which systems and teams use the information; where costs, value, and risk accumulate; and which participants must cooperate for the intended outcome.', detail: 'Terra Nexus helps clients identify the true control points and gaps that determine whether a program can operate.' },
    { title: 'Design the Supplier and Producer Proposition', body: 'Suppliers and producers need clear requirements, economics, support, and value. A program may involve premiums, cost sharing, preferred status, technical support, long-term commitments, data payments, financing, outcome payments, product access, or other incentives.', detail: 'Terra Nexus helps structure eligibility, enrollment, evidence, contracts, incentives, escalation, corrective action, and the treatment of existing suppliers, new suppliers, incomplete data, and nonconforming volume.' },
    { title: 'Build Traceability, Chain of Custody, and Attribute Controls', body: 'The program must maintain a supportable relationship among physical product, transactions, data, calculations, and claims.', detail: 'Terra Nexus helps define product and attribute identifiers; eligible-volume rules; segregation, commingling, and mass-balance controls; transaction and inventory records; conversion and allocation factors; ledger and reconciliation processes; certificate or claim transfer; exception, correction, expiry, and reversal treatment; documentation retention and audit trails; and roles, approvals, and segregation of duties.' },
    { title: 'Connect Systems and Recurring Operations', body: 'Technology should support the operating model—not define it. Relevant systems may include procurement, ERP, farm management, traceability, LCA, carbon accounting, supplier management, quality, logistics, certification, customer reporting, and data warehouses.', detail: 'Terra Nexus helps clients define data architecture, integration, validation, version control, workflows, roles, quality checks, and exception management. We also help determine what should remain manual during a pilot and what should be automated at scale.' },
    { title: 'Launch, Reconcile, and Improve', body: 'Commercial programs require recurring work across procurement, operations, sustainability, finance, legal, technology, suppliers, customers, and assurance.', detail: 'Terra Nexus can support or manage supplier onboarding, evidence review, calculations, eligible-volume tracking, reconciliations, ledger management, partner coordination, corrective action, customer documentation, reporting, and audit readiness.' },
    { title: 'Connect Performance to the Right Commercial Pathway', body: 'The same underlying performance may create different value for different participants. Potential pathways may include responsible-sourcing and supplier-program claims, certification and chain-of-custody claims, Scope 3 reductions and insets, product footprints and product differentiation, low-carbon feedstock or regulatory value, environmental attributes or credits, procurement qualification and customer reporting, and resilience, service, risk, and supply-security value.', detail: 'Terra Nexus helps compare ownership, evidence, cost, market capacity, utilization, timing, acceptance risk, and participant economics.' },
  ],
  audiencePathways: [
    { title: 'Commodity Traders and Originators', description: 'Market and program strategy, producer and supplier engagement, origination, aggregation, traceability, environmental attributes, customer offerings, managed operations, controls, and diligence.' },
    { title: 'Ingredient and Feed Processors', description: 'Responsible sourcing, product and impact allocation, processing controls, co-product treatment, customer documentation, low-impact products, and claims.' },
    { title: 'Food and Beverage Companies', description: 'Supplier programs, product and portfolio sourcing, Scope 3, traceability, certification, customer claims, resilience, governance, and managed value-chain programs.' },
    { title: 'Retailers and Distributors', description: 'Category and supplier strategy, private-label sourcing, customer propositions, traceability, logistics, claims, food waste, and joint business planning.' },
    { title: 'Producers and Integrated Agricultural Companies', description: 'Program requirements, evidence, incentives, contracting, routes to market, producer economics, and supplier relationships.' },
    { title: 'Technology and Solution Providers', description: 'Product-market fit, workflow integration, data and model diligence, pilot design, partner strategy, enterprise adoption, and investor or customer support.' },
    { title: 'Investors and Environmental-Market Participants', description: 'Supply-chain, asset, program, technology, and claims diligence; operating-model assessment; value-creation planning; commercialization; and assurance readiness.' },
  ],
  decisionGroups: [
    { heading: 'Strategy, Sourcing & Commercial Leaders', items: ['Which products, suppliers, sourcing regions, and impacts should be prioritized?', 'What level of traceability is required for the decision or claim?', 'Which chain-of-custody model fits the product and market?', 'How should supplier costs, premiums, incentives, and value be allocated?'] },
    { heading: 'Operations, Data & Claims Teams', items: ['Which data must originate upstream and which can be calculated downstream?', 'How will materials, attributes, and claims be reconciled through processing?', 'Who owns each attribute and who may use it?', 'What evidence and controls are proportionate to the commercial value?'] },
    { heading: 'Governance & Risk Teams', items: ['How should procurement, sustainability, operations, finance, technology, legal, and sales share accountability?', 'How will the program manage exceptions, unsupported supply, and changing requirements?'] },
  ],
  workstreams: [
    { title: 'Evaluate the Opportunity', items: ['Supply-chain, market, customer, and regulatory assessment', 'Sourcing-region and supplier prioritization', 'Business cases, economics, and value-allocation scenarios', 'Technology, program, partner, project, and transaction diligence', 'Build-buy-partner and operating-model decisions'] },
    { title: 'Design the Program', items: ['Supplier and producer propositions', 'Sourcing, traceability, chain-of-custody, and claims architecture', 'Roles, incentives, contracts, governance, and controls', 'Product, environmental-attribute, and customer pathways', 'Pilot and scale strategy'] },
    { title: 'Build the Infrastructure', items: ['Workflows, systems, data models, and integration requirements', 'Transaction, inventory, allocation, and ledger controls', 'Quality, exception, correction, and document-retention processes', 'Supplier and partner onboarding', 'Customer, certification, and assurance documentation'] },
    { title: 'Launch and Scale', items: ['Pilot delivery and decision gates', 'Supplier and producer enrollment', 'Training and change management', 'First transactions, reconciliations, and claims packages', 'Expansion across products, facilities, geographies, and customers'] },
    { title: 'Manage Delivery', items: ['Recurring data and evidence review', 'Eligible-volume and attribute tracking', 'Partner, supplier, and customer coordination', 'Reconciliation and exception management', 'Reporting, audit readiness, and continuous improvement'] },
  ],
  valuePathways: [
    { label: 'Responsible-sourcing and supplier-program claims' },
    { label: 'Certification and chain-of-custody claims' },
    { label: 'Scope 3 reductions and insets' },
    { label: 'Product footprints and product differentiation' },
    { label: 'Low-carbon feedstock or regulatory value' },
    { label: 'Environmental attributes or credits' },
    { label: 'Procurement qualification and customer reporting' },
    { label: 'Resilience, service, risk, and supply-security value' },
  ],
  validationRows: [
    { category: 'Desirability', pitfall: 'The claim is selected before the physical and commercial flow is understood.', validation: 'Understand the physical and commercial flow before selecting the claim it will support.' },
    { category: 'Viability', pitfall: 'Suppliers receive requirements without a viable economic proposition.', validation: 'Give suppliers a viable economic proposition alongside sustainability requirements.' },
    { category: 'Viability', pitfall: 'Traceability is more complex than the value it protects.', validation: 'Match traceability complexity to the value it protects — no more, no less.' },
    { category: 'Feasibility', pitfall: 'Chain-of-custody rules do not match actual storage, mixing, or processing.', validation: 'Design chain-of-custody rules to match actual storage, mixing, and processing operations.' },
    { category: 'Feasibility', pitfall: 'Technology is purchased before roles and workflows are defined.', validation: 'Define roles and workflows before purchasing technology.' },
    { category: 'Feasibility', pitfall: 'Product, inventory, carbon, certification, and financial systems use different boundaries.', validation: 'Align product, inventory, carbon, certification, and financial systems on shared boundaries.' },
    { category: 'Strategic Fit', pitfall: 'Contracts do not clearly allocate attributes, representations, or liabilities.', validation: 'Allocate attributes, representations, and liabilities clearly in every contract.' },
    { category: 'Feasibility', pitfall: 'Processing conversion and co-product allocation are poorly controlled.', validation: 'Control processing conversion and co-product allocation before making claims.' },
    { category: 'Feasibility', pitfall: 'Reconciliation and exception management are underestimated.', validation: 'Resource reconciliation and exception management as core operating costs.' },
    { category: 'Feasibility', pitfall: 'The pilot is designed but recurring operations are not staffed or governed.', validation: 'Staff and govern recurring operations before scaling beyond the pilot.' },
  ],
  ctaHeading: 'Build a Supply Chain That Can Deliver and Substantiate Value',
  ctaBody: 'Whether the starting point is responsible sourcing, a producer program, Scope 3, a differentiated product, regulatory eligibility, traceability, resilience, or a customer requirement, Terra Nexus helps clients build the complete path from upstream performance to credible market value.',
  ctaButton: { label: 'Discuss Your Supply Chain', href: '/contact/' },
};
